# Coefficient formula for the integration class

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.integration_cochain_integral_class`
(`42f3e2ca-8754-4fed-b68b-1fb792825242`), by davidloeffler, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `f054d112-c61a-4502-8531-010fe8217466`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

Let $N>0$ and $k\ge 2$. For the cusp-to-cusp integration class $I(f)$, evaluation on the divisor
$[\infty]-[r]$ recovers the normalized vertical modular integrals. For every rational cusp $r$ and
every $0\le j\le k-2$,

$$
\operatorname{ev}_{j,r}(I(f))=\binom{k-2}{j}\,2\pi\int_0^\infty f(r+it)(r+it)^j\,dt.
$$

Thus the cohomology class attached to $f$ satisfies the predicate `IntegralClass f` with the
prescribed binomial coefficient and $-2\pi i$ integration normalization.

## Explanation of the source proof

This is the bookkeeping half of the integral-class property of the integration cochain; no analysis
is involved.

Recall the definitions. For a cusp form $f\in S_k(\Gamma_1(N))$ and $r\in\mathbf Q$ the cusp period
polynomial is
$$P_f(r)=\sum_{j=0}^{k-2}\binom{k-2}{j}\,\Big(2\pi\int_0^\infty
f(r+it)\,(r+it)^j\,dt\Big)\,X^jY^{k-2-j},$$
the primitive is $P_f$ on $\mathbf Q$ and $0$ at $\infty$, and the integration cochain is
$\Phi_f(x,y)=P_f(y)-P_f(x)$. The evaluation `evaluation j r` of a class $\phi$ is the coefficient of
$X^jY^{k-2-j}$ in $\phi(\infty,r)$, and `IntegralClass f φ` asks that this coefficient equal
$\binom{k-2}{j}$ times the modular integral $2\pi\int_0^\infty f(r+it)(r+it)^j\,dt$ for every $j\le
k-2$ and $r\in\mathbf Q$.

**Proof.** If $(I f)$ has underlying cochain $\Phi_f$, then
$(If)(\infty,r)=P_f(r)-P_f(\infty)=P_f(r)$ (`integrationCochain_infty`). The coefficient of
$X^jY^{k-2-j}$ in the finite sum defining $P_f(r)$ is obtained from `MvPolynomial.coeff_sum` and
`coeff_monomial`: the monomial exponent vectors `binaryExponent (k-2) i` for $0\le i\le k-2$ are
pairwise distinct, since their first coordinate is $i$ (`binaryExponent_apply_zero`), so only the
term $i=j$ survives (`Finset.sum_eq_single`), and it contributes exactly $\binom{k-2}{j}$ times the
modular integral (`coeff_cuspPeriodPolynomial`). This is the required identity for every $j\le k-2$
and every $r$.

